{
  "_doc": "Zimmermann et al. 2019 (Nature 570:462-467, PMID 31158845): the 271-drug x 76-strain metabolism screen, read from the single supplementary workbook on the Springer static host. This is D8's OTHER direction and the reason the junction edges hang off Taxon rather than Drug: Maier's screen says the drug changes the bug, this one says the bug changes the drug, and Part D required in advance that the two land as different edge types because collapsing them conflates antimicrobial killing with drug metabolism. Every cell of the matrix was measured, so this fragment declares TWO relationships and not one: METABOLISES for the 2,575 hits and DOES_NOT_METABOLISE for the 17,479 measured non-hits. Four of the screen's eighty measured columns are Control pH 4-7 abiotic-degradation controls sitting between two strain columns with the same five sub-columns each; they are excluded in the prep and ledgered, which is what makes the paper's own 76 strains reproduce instead of 80. Drug is declared here as well as in chembl.json and maier2018.json because this source mints nodes for the 23 screened compounds no join route reaches, keyed on the screened name; its three cas / trade_name / therapeutic_indication columns are empty on every row the other two wrote, which is what microbiomekg.tables.Writer's merge is for. There is no Gene node: supplementary table 13's 30 gene products ride on the edge as gene_locus_tags / gene_products / gene_protein_ids, because they were identified in a gain-of-function library expressed in E. coli rather than in the 76 strains these edges come from (docs/model.md).",
